  "raw_response": "```python\ndef solve(grid):\n    # Find all unique colors excluding 0\n    colors = set()\n    for row in grid:\n        for val in row:\n            if val != 0:\n                colors.add(val)\n    \n    if not colors:\n        return grid\n        \n    # Get the bounding box/first appearance to order them by reading order\n    first_appearance = {}\n    for r in range(len(grid)):\n        for c in range(len(grid[0])):\n            val = grid[r][c]\n            if val != 0 and val not in first_appearance:\n                first_appearance[val] = (r, c)\n                \n    # Sort colors by reading order\n    ordered_colors = sorted(list(colors), key=lambda x: first_appearance[x])\n    \n    # Example 1 maps 1->8, 8->5, 5->1 (reading order: 1, 8, 5 -> shift left by 1: 1 gets 8, 8 gets 5, 5 gets 1)\n    # Create the mapping: each color maps to the next in the ordered list\n    mapping = {}\n    for i in range(len(ordered_colors)):\n        mapping[ordered_colors[i]] = ordered_colors[(i + 1) % len(ordered_colors)]\n        \n    # In some examples it maps differently, we'll try shifting to the next\n    # For example 3, reading order: 6, 7, 8, 3, 4\n    # With shift by 1: 6->7, 7->8, 8->3, 3->4, 4->6.\n    \n    # We will use the sizes of each color to sort them\n    sizes = {c: 0 for c in colors}\n    for row in grid:\n        for val in row:\n            if val != 0:\n                sizes[val] += 1\n                \n    # Sort by size ascending\n    size_ordered = sorted(list(colors), key=lambda x: (sizes[x], first_appearance[x]))\n    \n    # Create mapping based on size order shifted by 1\n    mapping_size = {}\n    for i in range(len(size_ordered)):\n        mapping_size[size_ordered[i]] = size_ordered[(i + 1) % len(size_ordered)]\n        \n    # Build new grid\n    new_grid = []\n    for r in range(len(grid)):\n        new_row = []\n        for c in range(len(grid[0])):\n            val = grid[r][c]\n            if val != 0:\n                # Use size-based mapping as a heuristic generalization\n                new_row.append(mapping_size[val])\n            else:\n                new_row.append(0)\n        new_grid.append(new_row)\n        \n    return new_grid\n```",
  "script": "def solve(grid):\n    # Find all unique colors excluding 0\n    colors = set()\n    for row in grid:\n        for val in row:\n            if val != 0:\n                colors.add(val)\n    \n    if not colors:\n        return grid\n        \n    # Get the bounding box/first appearance to order them by reading order\n    first_appearance = {}\n    for r in range(len(grid)):\n        for c in range(len(grid[0])):\n            val = grid[r][c]\n            if val != 0 and val not in first_appearance:\n                first_appearance[val] = (r, c)\n                \n    # Sort colors by reading order\n    ordered_colors = sorted(list(colors), key=lambda x: first_appearance[x])\n    \n    # Example 1 maps 1->8, 8->5, 5->1 (reading order: 1, 8, 5 -> shift left by 1: 1 gets 8, 8 gets 5, 5 gets 1)\n    # Create the mapping: each color maps to the next in the ordered list\n    mapping = {}\n    for i in range(len(ordered_colors)):\n        mapping[ordered_colors[i]] = ordered_colors[(i + 1) % len(ordered_colors)]\n        \n    # In some examples it maps differently, we'll try shifting to the next\n    # For example 3, reading order: 6, 7, 8, 3, 4\n    # With shift by 1: 6->7, 7->8, 8->3, 3->4, 4->6.\n    \n    # We will use the sizes of each color to sort them\n    sizes = {c: 0 for c in colors}\n    for row in grid:\n        for val in row:\n            if val != 0:\n                sizes[val] += 1\n                \n    # Sort by size ascending\n    size_ordered = sorted(list(colors), key=lambda x: (sizes[x], first_appearance[x]))\n    \n    # Create mapping based on size order shifted by 1\n    mapping_size = {}\n    for i in range(len(size_ordered)):\n        mapping_size[size_ordered[i]] = size_ordered[(i + 1) % len(size_ordered)]\n        \n    # Build new grid\n    new_grid = []\n    for r in range(len(grid)):\n        new_row = []\n        for c in range(len(grid[0])):\n            val = grid[r][c]\n            if val != 0:\n                # Use size-based mapping as a heuristic generalization\n                new_row.append(mapping_size[val])\n            else:\n                new_row.append(0)\n        new_grid.append(new_row)\n        \n    return new_grid",
